{"qid":"Q733","names":["Paraguay"],"title":"Paraguay","endonym":{"native":"República del Paraguay","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Paraguay declared independence from Spain on 14–15 May 1811, making it one of the first South American republics, and the early republic was shaped by the autocratic isolationism of José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia (El Supremo, 1814–1840), who closed borders and nationalized church property to build a self-sufficient state. The catastrophic War of the Triple Alliance (1864–1870), in which Paraguay fought simultaneously against Brazil, Argentina, and Uruguay under Francisco Solano López, resulted in the deaths of an estimated 60–70 percent of the country's population and the loss of large border territories. The twentieth century brought repeated military coups, culminating in the 35-year dictatorship of Alfredo Stroessner (1954–1989), whose overthrow initiated a democratic transition confirmed by the 1992 constitution. Paraguay's economy has since grown significantly around hydroelectric power (Itaipú Dam, opened 1984) and soy and beef exports.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Post-1870 boundaries are defined by the post-war treaties (Treaty of Petropolis 1903, final settlements with Bolivia after the Chaco War 1938); the Chaco region boundary was disputed and only settled in 1938, so pre-1938 western extents carry some uncertainty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Paraguay","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Paraguay"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Paraguay","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraguay"}],"britannica":"place/Paraguay","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Paragua